<h2>Start with registrations, not price</h2>
<p>The first thing to check on any Indian exporter isn't their price — it's whether they're a real, registered business. Two registrations matter most for an agricultural export, and India's government publishes a way to check each of them:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>IEC (Import Export Code).</strong> Issued by India's DGFT, this is the base legal requirement to export from India. No legitimate exporter operates without one. Ask for it directly, and if a supplier is cagey about sharing it, that alone is worth pausing on.</li>
  <li><strong>APEDA registration.</strong> For agricultural and processed food exporters specifically. If you're buying fresh produce, grains, spices, or similar categories, this is the second thing to ask about.</li>
</ul>
<p>Two further records, GST registration and (for companies and LLPs) the corporate registry, help you confirm that the business behind those registrations is the one you're dealing with. The steps below say what to ask for and where to check it. None of them needs a paid service.</p>

<h3>Step 1: Check the IEC on DGFT's public lookup</h3>
<p><strong>Ask for:</strong> the IEC number and the exact registered firm name.<br>
<strong>Check it at:</strong> DGFT's <a href="https://www.dgft.gov.in/CP/?opt=view-any-ice" rel="noopener" target="_blank">View Any IEC</a> page. Enter the IEC and at least the first three characters of the firm name. If the portal replies that details for that IEC aren't available, either the number is wrong or it doesn't belong to that name. The same page shows whether the IEC is on DGFT's Denied Entity List, which you want to see clear.</p>

<h3>Step 2: Confirm APEDA registration (RCMC) for farm products</h3>
<p><strong>Ask for:</strong> the APEDA RCMC (Registration-cum-Membership Certificate) number and a copy of the certificate.<br>
<strong>Check it at:</strong> APEDA's <a href="https://apeda.gov.in/exporter-status" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Exporter Status</a> page, which searches by RCMC number or exporter name. APEDA's <a href="https://apeda.gov.in/exporter-directory" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Exporter's Directory</a> can also be filtered by state, product and exporter type (manufacturer, merchant, or both), which helps you see whether a supplier's claimed role matches its listing. Since July 2023 APEDA issues RCMCs through the DGFT portal, and an IEC is a prerequisite, so the firm name on the RCMC should match the name on the IEC.</p>

<h3>Step 3: Look up the GSTIN</h3>
<p><strong>Ask for:</strong> the exporter's GSTIN, which should also appear on their invoices.<br>
<strong>Check it at:</strong> the GST portal's <a href="https://services.gst.gov.in/services/searchtp" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Search Taxpayer</a> page, which takes a GSTIN and needs no login. Compare the legal name and registration status it returns with the IEC and the proforma invoice. A GSTIN that returns a different business name is a serious warning.</p>

<h3>Step 4: Check company records where they apply</h3>
<p><strong>Ask for:</strong> the Corporate Identification Number (CIN) or LLPIN, if the exporter is a company or LLP.<br>
<strong>Check it at:</strong> the Ministry of Corporate Affairs <a href="https://www.mca.gov.in/content/mca/global/en/mca/master-data/MDS.html" rel="noopener" target="_blank">master data</a> service, which currently asks you to log in to the MCA portal first. Proprietorships and partnership firms aren't registered with MCA, so their absence isn't a problem in itself.</p>

<h3>Step 5: Make sure every name matches</h3>
<p>The single most useful test ties the steps together. The business name on the IEC, the APEDA record, the GST registration, the proforma invoice and the beneficiary on the bank account you're asked to pay should be the same entity. A request to pay a personal account, or an unrelated company "handling payments for us", deserves a direct question and a written explanation before any money moves.</p>

